    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks. To be more specific, I'm seeing a lot of cases involving lesions that present with very strange, almost \"gnarled\" or \"knobby\" textures that don't seem to follow the usual progression of common growths. When the texture is that irregular—almost like it's breaking the normal symmetry of the skin—is there a specific sub-category for \"atypical morphology\" that takes precedence over just labeling it an unspecified lesion? I want to avoid the denials for lack of specificity.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ICD-10 Task Force guidelines generally prioritize specificity when available, and there is a hierarchy in taxonomy where you should exhaust all route-specific and category-specific codes before resorting to unspecified codes. In the case of complicated moles or lesions with atypical texture, it may be necessary to consult the Alphabetic Index first to see if there’s a more specific code or if a code above or below the mole nevi category could apply. If no closer match is found, and this uniquely textured lesion does not fit any specific category, you might need to use a general code denoting the abnormal appearance while paying attention to any associated diagnoses linked with skin conditions, such as actinic keratoses or cutaneous horns, provided these diagnoses indicate an abnormal growth pattern. If this approach doesn't yield specificity, an unspecified code might be used with a descriptive attachment explaining the unusual presentation. Always make sure to document your clinical reasoning thoroughly and consult up-to-date coding resources or professionals if clarity continues to be an issue. Moreover, ICD-10 also allows for descriptive notes in the diagnosis section, which can be helpful for atypical cases.",
      "source": "target",
