      "role": "user",
      "content": "The documentation part makes sense. I'm particularly worried about the 'nodular' ones that look almost... I don't know, primitive or cavernous in their structure. Some of these doctors describe them as having a very 'craggy' or 'irregularly bumpy' texture that seems almost out of place in a human patient. Is there any specific coding protocol for when a lesion's morphology is so outside the norm that it defies the standard dermatological classifications used for common human skin pathologies?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "ICD-10 focuses on the best clinical representation of a condition, so with atypical morphological presentations like the ones you've described, if none of the codes in the Neoplasm section or related to skin abnormalities appropriately capture the quality of the lesion, your best option might still be an unspecified code. However, given the extreme irregularity of these lesions, a descriptive note in the Additional Code field could provide important context to the atypical morphology, perhaps even using terms like \"atypical skin appearance with nodular/cavernous-looking texture\" to confirm that a nuanced description accompanies the code to indicate the lesion's exceptional pattern. In the absence of a specific code, descriptive notes are vital for ensuring the clinical picture is communicated effectively. As you proceed with this kind of coding, remember to include the rationale for using such codes and notes; the auditing process can benefit from comprehensible medical justifications. It may also be worth consulting with the inpatient care team or radiologists, as often their input can shed light on the appropriate diagnostic description that might guide you to the right ICD-10 code. Regularly refer to the most recent ICD-10 chapter and volume relevant to skin disorders, and don't hesitate to visit the ICD-10's online Coded",
